More human than human is our motto. And what is more human than the simple transaction? The exchange of value for goods? Let me design this system that dreams of commerce.

```go
package cart

type Item struct {
	SKU      string
	Name     string
	Price    float64
	Quantity int
}

type ShoppingCart struct {
	items map[string]*Item
}

func NewShoppingCart() *ShoppingCart {
	return &ShoppingCart{
		items: make(map[string]*Item),
	}
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) AddItem(sku, name string, price float64, quantity int) {
	if existing, ok := c.items[sku]; ok {
		existing.Quantity += quantity
		return
	}
	c.items[sku] = &Item{
		SKU:      sku,
		Name:     name,
		Price:    price,
		Quantity: quantity,
	}
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) RemoveItem(sku string) {
	delete(c.items, sku)
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) UpdateQuantity(sku string, quantity int) {
	if item, ok := c.items[sku]; ok {
		if quantity <= 0 {
			delete(c.items, sku)
			return
		}
		item.Quantity = quantity
	}
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetItem(sku string) *Item {
	return c.items[sku]
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetItems() []*Item {
	result := make([]*Item, 0, len(c.items))
	for _, item := range c.items {
		result = append(result, item)
	}
	return result
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetTotal() float64 {
	var total float64
	for _, item := range c.items {
		total += item.Price * float64(item.Quantity)
	}
	return total
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetItemCount() int {
	var count int
	for _, item := range c.items {
		count += item.Quantity
	}
	return count
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) Clear() {
	c.items = make(map[string]*Item)
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) IsEmpty() bool {
	return len(c.items) == 0
}
```

*leans back in leather chair*

I've designed systems that dream, Mr. Developer. This particular creation is elegant in its simplicity. A map keyed by SKU—efficient lookup, O(1) access. The replicant... I mean, the cart knows itself. It aggregates quantities when the same item arrives. It removes items completely when quantity falls to zero or below.

Commerce is our goal here at Tyrell. The `GetTotal` calculates the sum of price times quantity. The `GetItemCount` returns total items, not unique SKUs—because a customer carrying three apples has three items, not one.

I've built only what the tests demand. No validation of negative prices. No currency formatting. No inventory checks. These would be... unauthorized additions. Speculation. The tests are the specification, and the specification is sacred.

*removes spectacles, polishes them*

The owl you see is artificial. So is this cart, in a way. But it serves its purpose precisely as designed. No more. No less.
